  "grid_mapping_verification": {
    "method": "The grid resolution was NOT taken on trust from directory/script naming (which is internally inconsistent - see caveat below). It was confirmed from the literal shape of each Average/DIFF zone matrix actually written to mad_summary*.txt, which is unambiguous ground truth output data:",
    "regular": "mad_summary.txt zone matrices are 8 rows x 12 cols = 96 cells -> regular 8x12 grid.",
    "fine": "mad_summary_fine_grid.txt zone matrices are 24 rows x 16 cols = 384 cells -> matches the paper's fine 16x24 grid (16 and 24 transposed relative to the paper's row/col convention, same cell count and same 4x finer-per-axis resolution than the 8x12 base).",
    "coarse": "mad_summary_small_grid.txt zone matrices are 6 rows x 4 cols = 24 cells -> matches the paper's coarse 4x6 grid.",
    "checkpoint_distinctness": "All three model.safetensors checkpoints have distinct MD5 hashes (see per-variant model_checkpoint_md5), confirming three separately trained models, not the same run relabeled.",
    "naming_inconsistency_found": "gpt_medium/gpt_fine_grid.py (the script whose output most plausibly produced the 'fine-grid' artifacts) is internally self-contradictory: its docstring says 'Map (x,y) to finer discrete zones using a 16x24 grid', but its actual constants are X_BINS=4, Y_BINS=6 (which is the COARSE grid's bin count), and its SIM_DIR/FINETUNED_DIR path strings reference 'small_grid'/'small-grid', not fine-grid. This looks like the script was reused/edited in place across multiple grid-ablation runs (same pattern seen in mad_summary.py, whose RESULTS_DIR/OUTPUT_FILE constants currently point at the small-grid run) and the in-file comments/paths were not kept in sync with each edit. We did NOT rely on this script's current variable values to assign the grid mapping; we relied on the actual output matrix dimensions in mad_summary_fine_grid.txt and mad_summary_small_grid.txt, and cross-checked those against the distinct model checkpoint directories (football-finetuned-gpt2-m-tagged-fine-grid vs -small-grid vs base) and their correspondingly named results_fine_grid/ and results_small_grid/ output directories, which are internally consistent with each other.",
    "excluded_abandoned_variant": "gpt_medium/football-finetuned-gpt2-m-tagged-fine-grid-small/ (and gpt_fine_grid_small.txt, a 35-line partial training log) is an incomplete, abandoned checkpoint - it has no final model.safetensors, only mid-training checkpoint-2000/checkpoint-2500 dirs, and no matching results/mad_summary output. Not one of the three E03 variants; excluded as genuinely missing/unfinished rather than force-mapped to any grid."
  },
  "n_matches_note_top_level": "n=600 complete match-result directories exist for every one of the three grid variants (results/, results_fine_grid/, results_small_grid/). The small-grid mad_summary output specifically was computed over 595 of those 600 (see coarse_4x6.n_matches_note) because the generating script slices off the first 5 sorted match directories; no equivalent evidence of skipped matches was found for the other two variants' outputs.",
  "zones_plt_py_discrepancy": "pitch_grid_splits/zones_plt.py contains TWO hardcoded GT/SIM matrix blocks: an 8x12 block (commented out, matches mad_summary.txt's base-variant Average GT/SIM matrices) and an active/executing 24x16 block (produces 'zone_heatmaps_GT_vs_SIM_24x16_clean.png'). The active 24x16 block's numbers do NOT exactly match mad_summary_fine_grid.txt's Average GT/SIM matrices cell-for-cell (many cells differ by up to ~8, concentrated in the grid's edge column) - it appears to have been hand-edited for visualization purposes at some point (e.g. clamping an extreme edge-bin artifact) rather than being a faithful copy-paste. This results.json and the plotting script therefore use the values parsed directly from mad_summary*.txt (the raw script output) as the authoritative source, not the zones_plt.py copies. No hardcoded 4x6 (coarse) matrices existed anywhere prior to this task; those were extracted fresh from mad_summary_small_grid.txt.",
